const physicsStories=[
{id:'moving-earth',title:'What if the observer is moving?',category:'THE CHANGING SKY',scientist:'copernicus',glyph:'⊕ → ☉',teaser:'A planet seems to reverse course. Perhaps the surprise is in our own motion.',paragraphs:[
'Watch a slower vehicle through the window as you overtake it. For a moment, it seems to slip backward. Earth’s motion offers a similar way to understand the apparent backward motion of an outer planet against the stars.',
'Copernicus placed Earth among the planets orbiting the Sun. This changed the explanation: the observer was part of the moving system. His model still used circular motions; it was not yet the elliptical picture we use today.',
'In the experiment, follow the line from Earth to the outer planet as Earth overtakes it. The interesting question is no longer just “Where is that planet?” It is also “Where are we looking from?”'],sources:[['NASA · The history of planetary motion','https://science.nasa.gov/earth/earth-observatory/planetary-motion/']]},
{id:'venus',title:'Venus had a story to tell',category:'EVIDENCE CHANGES IDEAS',scientist:'galileo',glyph:'◐',teaser:'A telescope revealed phases that an old arrangement of the planets could not explain.',paragraphs:[
'In 1610, Galileo turned his telescope toward Venus and found that it changed phase, much like the Moon. A bright point of light had become a world with a visible geometry.',
'The observed sequence of phases was incompatible with the traditional Ptolemaic arrangement of Venus always staying between Earth and the Sun. It supported Venus traveling around the Sun.',
'That did not, by itself, prove that Earth moved. Tycho’s hybrid system could also accommodate Venus orbiting the Sun. The lesson is useful far beyond astronomy: evidence can rule out one explanation without selecting a unique replacement. Below, visit Galileo’s separate falling-body experiment, or compare the historical sky models in the atlas.'],sources:[['Museo Galileo · Phases of Venus','https://catalogue.museogalileo.it/indepth/PhasesVenus.html']]},
{id:'kepler-mars',title:'Mars refused to be a perfect circle',category:'MODELS MEET MEASUREMENTS',scientist:'kepler',glyph:'a ≠ b',teaser:'Kepler’s difficult planet helped turn a beautiful assumption into a testable law.',paragraphs:[
'Tycho Brahe collected exceptionally careful planetary observations. Kepler inherited a hard problem: make sense of Mars. A circular orbit was an attractive starting point, but the observations demanded more.',
'Kepler eventually described planets moving on ellipses, with the Sun at a focus. Their speed also changes: a planet sweeps out equal areas in equal times, moving faster when closer to the Sun.',
'The result matters because the data were allowed to change the shape of the model. In the orbit experiment, increase eccentricity and watch the unequal speeds. The path is not merely a stretched circle with a dot running around it at a constant pace.'],sources:[['NASA · Orbits and Kepler’s laws','https://science.nasa.gov/solar-system/orbits-and-keplers-laws/']]},
{id:'joule-paddle',title:'A paddle wheel rewrote the story of heat',category:'THERMODYNAMICS',scientist:'joule',glyph:'W → ΔT',teaser:'Falling weights, stirred water, and a tiny temperature rise connected two worlds.',paragraphs:[
'Joule used falling weights to turn a paddle wheel in water. The temperature increase was small, but it was measurable. Mechanical work had produced heating.',
'This was part of a larger change in how heat was understood: work and heating could be connected through a quantitative account of energy, rather than treated as unrelated phenomena.',
'Our miniature version gives the water a fixed amount of work. Change its mass and watch the temperature rise change. The same energy spread through more water produces a smaller temperature increase. The animation is idealized; the historical challenge was making delicate measurements while accounting for unwanted heat exchange.'],sources:[['Science and Industry Museum · James Joule’s energy experiments','https://blog.scienceandindustrymuseum.org.uk/james-joules-energy/']]},
{id:'carnot-limit',title:'The engine you cannot out-engineer',category:'THERMODYNAMICS',scientist:'carnot',glyph:'η < 1',teaser:'Some limits are written into the temperatures, not the workmanship.',paragraphs:[
'Imagine removing every avoidable loss from a heat engine. Would all the incoming heat become work? Carnot’s insight tells us that even a reversible engine operating between two reservoirs has a ceiling.',
'In modern notation, that ceiling is one minus the cold temperature divided by the hot temperature, with both temperatures measured in kelvin. If the reservoirs are 300 K and 600 K, the maximum efficiency is 50 percent.',
'Real engines fall below that bound. In the experiment, keep the cold side fixed and raise the hot side. The useful lesson is the distinction between an engineering problem that can be improved and a physical limit that better machining cannot remove.'],sources:[['MIT · Explained: The Carnot limit','https://news.mit.edu/2010/explained-carnot-0519']]},
{id:'uncertainty',title:'Uncertainty is not a broken ruler',category:'QUANTUM PHYSICS',scientist:'heisenberg',glyph:'Δx Δp',teaser:'A quantum state can be narrow in position or momentum, but not arbitrarily narrow in both.',paragraphs:[
'It is tempting to imagine that every measurement problem can be solved with a finer instrument. Quantum uncertainty asks us to reconsider the state we are measuring, not just the tool.',
'For position and momentum, the spreads of their probability distributions obey a lower bound. A state sharply localized in position needs a broader mixture of momentum components. Heisenberg’s uncertainty relations became a central part of the break with classical descriptions.',
'The paired Gaussian plots show a minimum-uncertainty example. Narrow the position distribution and the momentum distribution widens. The glowing dots only guide the eye along the curves; they are not hidden particle trajectories. This is a relationship between distributions, not a claim that nothing can be known.'],sources:[['Werner Heisenberg · Nobel lecture','https://www.nobelprize.org/uploads/2018/06/heisenberg-lecture.pdf']]},
{id:'faraday',title:'From a bookbinder’s bench to electric fields',category:'EXPERIMENTAL CURIOSITY',scientist:'faraday',glyph:'−dΦ/dt',teaser:'Faraday’s path into science began far from a conventional academic career.',paragraphs:[
'Michael Faraday trained as a bookbinder before becoming one of the great experimental investigators of electricity and magnetism. His work at the Royal Institution ranged from electromagnetic rotation to induction and the relation between light and magnetism.',
'The 1831 discovery of electromagnetic induction connected a changing magnetic environment with electrical effects. That connection underlies generators and transformers.',
'The experiment here draws magnetic flux and induced voltage. Turn up the frequency: a faster change produces a larger voltage. Then try the magnet-and-coil game. The important ingredient is change; simply leaving a magnet still is not the same thing. A compact experiment can reveal a relationship that a static picture hides.'],sources:[['Royal Institution · Michael Faraday','https://www.rigb.org/explore-science/explore/person/michael-faraday-1791-1867']]},
{id:'fission',title:'The clue was a much smaller atom',category:'NUCLEAR PHYSICS',scientist:'meitner',glyph:'Δm c²',teaser:'A chemical result needed a new physical interpretation.',paragraphs:[
'Experiments by Otto Hahn and Fritz Strassmann produced a startling clue: bombarding uranium with neutrons could yield much lighter elements. Lise Meitner and Otto Frisch supplied the physical interpretation in terms of a nucleus splitting.',
'The energy release can be understood through the mass difference between the initial and final systems. Nuclear binding energy makes a small mass difference correspond to a large amount of energy.',
'The animation shows just one possible channel, not a universal set of products. Follow the incoming neutron, the fragments, and the emitted neutrons. The broader story is about complementary kinds of evidence: identifying the products chemically and explaining the process physically were both essential.'],sources:[['Otto Hahn · Nobel lecture, including Meitner and Frisch’s interpretation','https://www.nobelprize.org/uploads/2018/06/hahn-lecture.pdf']]}
];
Object.assign(titles,{notes:'Physics stories',story:'Story reader'});
function storyCard(s,i){return `<button class="story-card" data-story="${s.id}"><span class="meta">${String(i+1).padStart(2,'0')} / ${s.category}</span><span class="story-glyph">${s.glyph}</span><h3>${s.title}</h3><p>${s.teaser}</p><span class="visit">1 MIN READ · THEN EXPERIMENT ↗</span></button>`}
function renderStories(el){el.innerHTML=`<span class="eyebrow">THE READING ROOM / STORIES BEHIND THE EQUATIONS</span><h2>Ideas have backstories.</h2><p class="lede">Short reads about observations, stubborn questions, and experiments that changed the picture. Each story includes its sources and a way to explore the physics.</p><div class="story-grid">${physicsStories.map(storyCard).join('')}</div>`}
function openStory(id){const story=physicsStories.find(s=>s.id===id);if(!story)return;const el=openApp('story').querySelector('.window-content');el.innerHTML=`<div class="atlas-nav"><button data-open="notes">‹ All physics stories</button><span>1 MIN READ</span></div><article class="story-article"><span class="eyebrow">${story.category}</span><h2>${story.title}</h2><p class="story-deck">${story.teaser}</p>${story.paragraphs.map(p=>`<p>${p}</p>`).join('')}<div class="story-sources"><h3>Read the source</h3>${story.sources.map(([label,url])=>`<a href="${url}" target="_blank" rel="noopener">${label} ↗</a>`).join('')}</div><button class="primary" data-scientist="${story.scientist}">Explore the related experiment ↗</button></article>`;el.scrollTop=0}
document.addEventListener('click',e=>{const a=e.target.closest('[data-story]');if(a)openStory(a.dataset.story)});
